i once posted a system of credit for nethack using credit cards and
so on. i liked the idea but by the implications of credit cards was
all wrong! in fact, there should not be credit cards in the dungeon
since lock picks and skeleton keys fill those roles already. a new
proposal for a credit scheme set in a medieval milieu ...

occasionally, throughout the normal dungeon, an explorer |
will discover a royal treasury (shown at right) where    |    ----
one can deposit or withdraw gold. the royal treasurer is |    |@.|
there to conduct the transactions. the treasurer will do |    --+-
nothing for you (except spread mostly false rumors)      |
unless you are carrying an escutcheon that is _engraved_ |  royal treasury
with your name, which the treasurer will do for you when |
you first apply the small tool while standing in the chamber. the
treasurer could prompt you for the escutcheon. if your current name
is the same as that engraved on the escutcheon, you can do business:

* you may deposit as much gold as you like, however the gold is credited
    to the _person_ named on the escutcheon. a name change will cut you
    off from your money. escutcheons fade and your account disappears
    when you die. escutcheons are rare but found normally throughout
    the dungeon. there is a slight chance that a treasurer would sell
    you an escutcheon (1,000 gold pieces).

* at the time of the deposit, you could withdraw 1 gold doubloon (a small
    rock which cannot be created) for every 5,000 gold pieces in your
    account. the gold doubloon has unit weight.

* you may deposit doubloons (which stack) as you would gold pieces.

* you can withdraw gold or doubloons upto your account limit since your
    balance is maintained automagically throughout the dungeon. however,
    you are not given credit for deposited gold when you escape. you
    would recieve proper credit for doubloons in your possession when
    you escape.

* you can kill the treasurer (appropriate penalties for killing a
    friendly human), and collect whatever gold was left in the chamber
    however you will soon be dogged by a bounty hunter who would demand,
    at least, that amount of gold to stop pestering you. you could kill
    the human bounty hunter but he would be replaced by another with
    similar intentions. in addition to demanding payment, the bounty
    hunter would occasionally attack and sometimes steal something.

i think this can be the basis for a nethack-flavored credit scheme.

